request.description:
The QUERY_FEED half. The record must say QUERY_FEED and must name the query parameter, because a connector never guesses where the query goes.

It must also carry a named first-contact approval and an established robots position. The proposed record carries NEITHER — deliberately, because this project has never contacted GDELT. Both are human acts:
  1. read https://api.gdeltproject.org/robots.txt and record the position;
  2. read and name GDELT’s terms;
  3. record a named first-contact approval (the SEC’s D17 does not cover this publisher);
  4. propose → approve → activate, with two people.
Until all four have happened this request halts the folder, which is the safe direction.

The assertions that separate a real mixed run from a fixture one, and a working architecture from a green tick.

A fixture run returns SUCCEEDED too, so what is checked here is PROVENANCE and SHAPE:
  - mode is LIVE and no fixture source id appears anywhere;
  - both live source ids appear in the coverage outcomes when they contributed one;
  - liveDiscovery reports what each connector put on the wire (D-060);
  - the SEC feeds are FIXED_FEED and issued ONE request each, whatever the plan’s size;
  - GDELT is QUERY_FEED, issued MORE than one request, and one distinct endpoint per request — which is what proves distinct planned queries were not collapsed;
  - GDELT’s request count never exceeds its recorded hourly budget;
  - two distinct publisher hosts were addressed.

liveDiscovery carries NO query text and NO URLs. Endpoint identity is counted through a one-way digest, because a planned query is the subject’s name plus a conduct term and an audit field is the wrong place for it.

